---
/**
 * One project card on `/development` — §10 items 6, 10.1, 10.2 and 10.3. Plan 05-09, task 2.
 *
 * ================================================================================================
 * THE ANCHOR STRUCTURE, AND WHY IT IS A STRETCHED LINK RATHER THAN A CARD-SHAPED ANCHOR
 * ================================================================================================
 *
 * §10.1 asks for two things that cannot both be literal HTML: the WHOLE CARD SURFACE links to
 * `project.href`, and `badges[]` is a row of outbound links. An `<a>` inside an `<a>` is invalid,
 * and the parser does not merely tolerate it — it CLOSES the outer anchor at the inner one, so the
 * badge row would silently fall outside the card link and the DOM would stop matching the source.
 *
 * Two structures were available. The card rendered AS an anchor with the badge row moved outside
 * it was rejected: the badges are part of the card's composition and moving them out of the
 * bordered box changes the reviewed design to satisfy an implementation constraint.
 *
 * So: the TITLE is the anchor, and it is stretched over the card with a positioned `::after`.
 * The accessibility reasoning, stated because it is the reason and not a side effect:
 *
 *   - The accessible NAME of the card's destination is the project title plus the visually-hidden
 *     "(opens in a new tab)". A card-shaped anchor would have taken the title, the status, the
 *     whole description and every tech chip into one link name — a screen-reader user would hear
 *     forty words to learn where one link goes.
 *   - There is exactly ONE anchor per card for the card's own destination, so the link list a
 *     screen reader builds has one entry per project rather than one entry plus a duplicate.
 *   - The badge links stay SIBLINGS of the stretched anchor, not descendants, so they are
 *     independently focusable and reachable, and tab order reads title → badges → next card.
 *   - The focus ring is drawn on the anchor's own inline box (`box-shadow` paints on the element,
 *     not on its `::after`), so a keyboard user sees a ring around the title rather than an
 *     invisible ring around the whole card.
 *
 * THE TRADEOFF, RECORDED RATHER THAN HIDDEN: the overlay sits above the description, so text
 * inside the card cannot be selected with the mouse. That is inherent to every stretched-link
 * implementation. It is accepted here because the card's text is a two-sentence summary that also
 * exists on the destination, and because the alternative structures cost more.
 *
 * ================================================================================================
 * `StatusPill`, NOT `Badge` (§10.2) — AND WHAT THE MAPPING ACTUALLY BUYS, MEASURED
 * ================================================================================================
 *
 * The generic path always renders a `<span>`; its own docstring names this exact surface: *"A
 * status read out of content is not a control — a Live / Maintained / Archived label on a public
 * Work card has nothing to activate."* `Badge`'s docstring points here for the three-way case.
 *
 * The tone mapping is §10.2's, unchanged. What it buys is narrower than §10.2 claims, and this was
 * MEASURED against the installed `2.0.0-beta.1` rather than read:
 *
 *     TONE_STEP (dist/chunk-TYSPT6SD.js)   muted: 1 · success: 1 · secondary: 2 · accent: 2 ·
 *                                          primary: 3 · danger: 3
 *
 * so `live`→`success` and `maintained`→`muted` both resolve to `data-step="1"`, which is the SAME
 * fill (`color-mix(--ink 7%, --paper)` in dark) and the SAME marker shape (hollow ring). The
 * shape-bearing split the docstring promises is therefore a TWO-way split on this page, not a
 * three-way one: `archived` separates by shape and fill, `live` and `maintained` separate only by
 * their marker hue (`--green` against `--ink-5`) and by their words. Reported as a finding rather
 * than worked around — §10.2's mapping is a reviewed decision and this component is not the place
 * to overrule it.
 *
 * ================================================================================================
 * `badges[]` HAS NO ICON, AND THAT IS A DESIGN-SYSTEM GAP RATHER THAN AN OMISSION
 * ================================================================================================
 *
 * §10.1 and this plan both ask for `badge.icon` mapped to a lucide icon from
 * `@akhil-saxena/design-system/icons`. MEASURED against the installed package: that subpath
 * exports 32 named wrappers and NONE of the four stored icon names has one —
 * `arrow-up-right`, `play-store`, `github` and `chrome-store` are all absent, there is no
 * `ExternalLink` or `ArrowUpRight`, and the generic `Icon` component (which takes a `LucideIcon`)
 * is not exported from any public subpath. lucide itself dropped brand glyphs at v1, so `Github`
 * and `Chrome` do not exist to re-export.
 *
 * Reaching around the design system into its transitive `lucide-react` was NOT taken: it is not a
 * dependency of this repository, and the Core Value settles exactly this trade. The badges
 * therefore render as text links carrying `badge.label` — which already reads "Play Store",
 * "GitHub", "Chrome Store" — and the gap is filed. `badges[]` is a LINK LIST and never the status.
 *
 * ================================================================================================
 * THE DESIGN SYSTEM'S INLINE STYLES, AND THE THREE OVERRIDES THAT FOLLOW FROM THEM
 * ================================================================================================
 *
 * MEASURED through `renderToStaticMarkup` with the installed build: `Link` emits
 * `style="font-family:var(--font);cursor:pointer"` on EVERY variant. An inline declaration cannot
 * be beaten by any app stylesheet rule, at any specificity, without `!important` — so a serif or
 * mono link is not expressible from CSS at all. `Link` DOES merge a consumer `style` object over
 * its own (measured: `fontFamily` was replaced and `cursor` retained), and it has a `color` prop,
 * so the overrides go through the component's own API rather than through `!important`.
 * This is why the card title carries a `style` and a `color` and the badges do not: the badges are
 * DM Sans, which is what `var(--font)` already resolves to.
 *
 * The badges take `variant="default"` and NOT `quiet` for the same reason, in reverse: `default` is
 * the stylesheet-only variant that already paints an underline, `--ink-2`, 1px thickness and a 2px
 * offset, so the design system supplies the whole affordance and this page overrides nothing. On a
 * card whose entire surface is already a link, a badge with no underline would be indistinguishable
 * from the words beside it — and the badge row is the only route to a project's source. §4.6b's
 * inline-`textDecorationColor` defect belongs to `footer` and `action`, not to `default`, so this
 * variant is also the one that is correct on a dark field.
 */

import { Card } from '@akhil-saxena/design-system/components/Card';
import { Chip } from '@akhil-saxena/design-system/components/Chip';
import { Link } from '@akhil-saxena/design-system/components/Link';
import type { Project } from '../../schemas';
import SocialIcons from './SocialIcons.astro';
/* The same separator `IdentityRail` uses for year ranges — one decision, not two that look alike. */
import { EN_DASH } from '../../lib/period';

interface Props {
  project: Project;
  /** `project.description` with `{{ds.*}}` already resolved — see `work.astro`. */
  description: string;
}

const { project, description } = Astro.props;

/*
 * ================================================================================================
 * NO STATUS PILL, AND `status` STAYS IN THE SCHEMA
 * ================================================================================================
 *
 * Akhil: *"remove pils for maintained/live etc."*
 *
 * WHAT WENT IS THE RENDERING, NOT THE FIELD. `status` is still REQUIRED on every project and is
 * still the only place currency lives (§13.1 rule 4) — Home's Act 2 and any future case-study page
 * read the same field, and a record that has stopped saying whether a thing is alive is a worse
 * record whatever this card draws. So `ProjectSchema` is untouched; only the pill, its tone map and
 * its derived label are gone from here.
 *
 * The pill was carrying less than it cost. Four of five projects read `Maintained`, so the column of
 * pills said "Maintained, Maintained, Maintained, Live" — a distinction between two states drawn
 * five times, ranged right where the eye lands first, in the one place on the card that competes
 * with the project's own name. The `Live` ones already announce it: Cairn and the design system are
 * the two cards with a globe in the dock, and a link to a running site is a stronger statement of
 * "this is live" than the word is.
 *
 * D-45 (StatusPill's unmapped-tone fallback) is now unreferenced by this page. It stays FILED: the
 * finding is about the component, not about this consumer, and the next page to use a pill inherits
 * it.
 */

/**
 * The announcement §10.1 requires, in one place so the anchors cannot drift apart.
 *
 * `.ds-visually-hidden` is the design system's own utility (`primitives.css`), so the string is
 * announced and never painted. Declared ONCE and used by the title link and by every badge's
 * `aria-label`, because two copies of an announcement drift and a drifted one is worse than none.
 */
const NEW_TAB = '(opens in a new tab)';

/**
 * The word a pending destination shows in place of its name.
 *
 * ONE CONSTANT, TWO PLACES — the visible label and the `title` — because they are the same claim and
 * two literals would drift. Not in `projects.json`: it is not a property of any project, it is what
 * this card says about a badge whose `pending` flag is set, and putting it in the data would invite
 * five spellings of it.
 */
const PENDING_LABEL = 'Coming Soon';

/**
 * The project's own artwork, and the two fields are NOT interchangeable.
 *
 * `icon` is STORE ARTWORK — an opaque square that looks the same on any ground, so one path is the
 * whole answer. `mark` is a THEME-AWARE PAIR for a knock-out mark whose cut-outs show the ground
 * through them, so it needs one file per theme and both are rendered with CSS choosing.
 *
 * Both are read here rather than in the template so the template asks one question per element. A
 * record carries at most one of them (measured across all five: three have `icon`, one has `mark`,
 * one has neither), but nothing in the schema enforces that and this does not assume it.
 */
const iconSrc = project.icon;
const mark = project.mark ?? null;

/*
 * ================================================================================================
 * `hostLabel` IS GONE, AND ITS JOB MOVED INTO THE DATA
 * ================================================================================================
 *
 * It derived a host from `project.href` for the one card with no icon, and the footer row now prints
 * `badge.label` for any badge whose icon is a globe instead. THAT IS A BETTER SOURCE, not just a
 * different one: a derived host is whatever the URL happens to say (`design-system-ed1.pages.dev`),
 * where a label is what Akhil decided the destination should be CALLED. The derivation was kept
 * through two arrangements "in case the dock loses its outbound mark"; it has now been replaced
 * rather than displaced, so it goes.
 */


/*
 * ================================================================================================
 * THE LIVE-COMPONENT STRIP IS GONE
 * ================================================================================================
 *
 * Akhil: *"in ds card, remove additional cip/statuspill, button, link etc."*
 *
 * It rendered a Chip, a StatusPill, a Button and a Link out of the installed package, on the theory
 * that the evidence and the thing it is evidence for should be the same objects. The theory was
 * sound and the RENDER was the problem: in a 420px two-up card the four specimens wrapped over
 * three ragged lines with `Link` stranded alone, and two of the four — `Button` and `Link` as
 * `as="span"` — read as unstyled text beside two real chips rather than as specimens. It also made
 * that one card the tallest in the grid, which set the matched height for all four.
 *
 * THE ARGUMENT MOVES TO ITS OWN PAGE. `/development/design-system` is where a component library can
 * be shown at the size a component library needs, and a strip of four atoms in a card was always a
 * trailer for it. `evidence` comes out of the schema and the data with this, because a field no
 * renderer reads is a field that drifts.
 */
---

<Card className="wk-card" variant="glass" padding="lg" hover>
  {
    /*
     * THE MARK IS A SIBLING OF THE HEAD, NOT A CHILD OF THE TITLE.
     *
     * It used to live inside the `<h3>`, which was right for a card — icon and name on one line —
     * and wrong for a ruled row, where the mark has a column of its own. The row layout reached it
     * with `display: contents` on the head AND the title, promoting grandchildren to grid items,
     * and that worked until it did not: with three columns sharing row tracks the status pill was
     * MEASURED 54 to 92px below the title, sized by whatever sat beside it in the body column.
     *
     * Hoisting the mark makes each column exactly one box — mark, head, body — so no two columns
     * share a row track and neither can push the other around. It also deletes both `display:
     * contents` rules, which were the clever part and are not missed.
     *
     * `alt=""` ON EVERY ONE: the link in the head names the project, and a mark announced as well
     * would make every row read its name twice. The `width`/`height` attributes give the browser
     * the RATIO before the file arrives, which is what stops the row reflowing as marks load.
     */
  }
  {iconSrc !== null && (
    <img class="wk-card-icon" src={iconSrc} alt="" width="30" height="30" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
  )}
  {mark !== null && (
    <>
      <img
        class="wk-card-icon wk-card-mark wk-card-mark-dark"
        src={mark.dark}
        alt=""
        width="30"
        height="30"
        loading="lazy"
        decoding="async"
      />
      <img
        class="wk-card-icon wk-card-mark wk-card-mark-light"
        src={mark.light}
        alt=""
        width="30"
        height="30"
        loading="lazy"
        decoding="async"
      />
    </>
  )}

  <div class="wk-card-head">
    <h3 class="wk-card-title">
      <Link
        className="wk-card-link"
        href={project.href}
        variant="quiet"
        color="var(--ink)"
        style={{ fontFamily: 'var(--font-display)' }}
        target="_blank"
        rel="noopener noreferrer"
      >
        {project.title}<span class="ds-visually-hidden"> {NEW_TAB}</span>
      </Link>
    </h3>
    {
      /*
       * ============================================================================================
       * THE OUTBOUND ARROW, WHICH APPEARS ON HOVER AND IS `aria-hidden` ALWAYS
       * ============================================================================================
       *
       * Akhil: *"when i hover on a card, i want a arrow on the top roight signifying that it
       * redirects to something the arrow can be hidden otherwise."*
       *
       * IT SITS WHERE THE STATUS PILL SAT, which is why the head still reads as a row: `.wk-card-head`
       * is `space-between` with the title left, and taking the pill out left that slot empty. So this
       * is not a new element in a new place — it is a better tenant for a slot the page already had.
       *
       * `aria-hidden`, AND THAT IS NOT AN OVERSIGHT. The title link already carries
       * `.ds-visually-hidden` "(opens in a new tab)" — see `NEW_TAB` above, which exists so every
       * outbound anchor announces itself exactly once. A screen reader hearing this arrow too would
       * hear the same fact twice per card. The arrow is a POINTER affordance for a fact the
       * non-visual path already states, which is the definition of decorative.
       *
       * IT IS NOT A LINK. Wrapping it in an anchor would add a sixth outbound anchor per card and a
       * second tab stop going exactly where the title already goes — `development.node.test.ts`
       * counts one per card plus one per badge and would fail, correctly. It is a glyph inside the
       * head, and the card's own stretched title link is what a click on it hits.
       */
    }
    <span class="wk-card-go" aria-hidden="true">
      <SocialIcons icon="arrow-up-right" size={14} />
    </span>
  </div>

  {
    /*
     * THE BODY IS ONE BOX, AND IT HAS TO BE.
     *
     * The ruled-row layout puts the mark in column 1, the name and its status in column 2 and this
     * in column 3. Placed as six separate grid items they shared ROW TRACKS with the name column —
     * MEASURED: the status pill landed 80px below the title, dragged down because row 2 was sized
     * by `cairn.co.in` sitting beside it in the next column. Two columns that must not affect each
     * other's vertical rhythm cannot be in the same row tracks, and no amount of CSS fixes that
     * from outside: it needs a box.
     *
     * It is also simpler. One `grid-column` on the wrapper replaces six, and the stack inside is a
     * flex column with one gap instead of six explicit `grid-row` numbers that had to be renumbered
     * whenever a card gained a line.
     */
  }
  {
    /*
     * ================================================================================================
     * THE DESTINATIONS ARE MARKS DOCKED UNDER THE PROJECT'S OWN MARK
     * ================================================================================================
     *
     * `Final Split Variations.html`, treatment A: a small row of store marks beneath each project
     * icon, in place of the text badges. Akhil: *"build a."*
     *
     * WHAT IT REPLACES: `Play Store   GitHub` as two underlined words in the card's body. Those had
     * to sit at the end of the description column, so a reader scanning the left edge for "where can
     * I get this" found the answer in a different place on every card, at the mercy of how long the
     * description ran. Docked under the mark they are always in the same place.
     *
     * THE ACCESSIBLE NAME IS THE BADGE'S LABEL, NOT THE MARK. Each is a real anchor with
     * `aria-label` — `Play Store (opens in a new tab)` — because a link whose only content is an
     * `aria-hidden` glyph is a link with no name. `title` is set too, so a sighted mouse user gets
     * the same word on hover that a screen-reader user hears.
     *
     * `.wk-marks` LIVES IN THE MARK'S GRID COLUMN, which is what makes "docked" true rather than
     * approximate — see `development.css`.
     */
  }

  <div class="wk-card-body">
    <p class="wk-card-desc">{description}</p>
  </div>

  {
    /*
     * ============================================================================================
     * THE FOOTER ROW — CHIPS LEFT, DESTINATION MARKS RIGHT, ON ONE LINE
     * ============================================================================================
     *
     * Akhil: *"for hued/momentum, etc, keep the placement for icons in smae line as pills."*
     *
     * The marks used to be docked in the card's grid under the project's own icon, and then pinned
     * absolutely to the bottom-right corner. Both put them on their OWN line, which cost a card ~24px
     * of height and left the chips row half empty beside them. One row carrying both is the same
     * information in one band: what it is built with on the left, where to get it on the right.
     *
     * IT IS A BOX, NOT TWO GRID CELLS. Two separate grid items on the same row would share a row
     * track with each other — the exact failure the three-block card was restructured to escape (see
     * the mark comment above: a status pill measured 54 to 92px away from its own title). A flex row
     * with `space-between` cannot do that: the marks are laid out against the chips and nothing else.
     *
     * `wk-card-foot` IS RENDERED EVEN WITH NO MARKS. Every project stores at least one badge
     * (`badges: z.array(BadgeSchema).min(1)`), so the right-hand side is never empty today; the row
     * exists unconditionally so a schema relaxation degrades to a left-aligned chip row rather than
     * to a missing element the stylesheet still targets.
     */
  }
  <div class="wk-card-foot">
    <ul class="wk-tags">
      {
        /* `Chip` is WRAPPED rather than given a `className`. §4.6c records the atom hook being
          clobbered by a consumer class; it CONCATENATES in the installed 2.0.0-beta.1 (measured), so
          the record is stale — but wrapping costs one element and cannot regress either way. */
      }
      {project.tech.map((tech) => <li><Chip>{tech}</Chip></li>)}
    </ul>

    {
      /*
       * ============================================================================================
       * A MARK IS A GLYPH, EXCEPT WHEN THE DESTINATION IS THE PRODUCT ITSELF
       * ============================================================================================
       *
       * Akhil: *"for cairn, write cairn.co.in with/without icon instead of just icon."*
       *
       * A Play Store triangle and the GitHub cat are UNIVERSAL — the glyph alone says where the link
       * goes, and a label beside it would be repeating the picture in words. A globe is not: it says
       * "a website" and nothing about which one, so on the one card whose destination is its own
       * running site the glyph was the least informative mark on the page. `cairn.co.in` says both
       * things at once.
       *
       * THE RULE IS ON THE ICON, NOT ON THE PROJECT ID. `badge.icon === 'globe'` means "this
       * destination has no recognisable mark of its own", which is the actual condition — an
       * `id === 'cairn'` branch would be right today and wrong the moment a second project ships a
       * site of its own. The label comes from the badge's own `label`, so the text is data.
       */
    }
    {
      /*
       * ============================================================================================
       * A PENDING DESTINATION IS TEXT, NOT A LINK
       * ============================================================================================
       *
       * Akhil: *"for momentum, on play store icon, add a tooltip on hover saying Coming Soon."*
       *
       * MEASURED, which is why this is a `<span>` and not a tooltip on an anchor: `momentum`'s Play
       * Store URL returns 404 while `hued`'s returns 200. The mark was a link to a Google error
       * page. A `title` tooltip would have told sighted mouse users and left keyboard, screen-reader
       * and touch readers clicking through to the 404 — it describes the problem rather than fixing
       * it.
       *
       * SO A PENDING BADGE RENDERS THE SAME GLYPH-PLUS-LABEL SHAPE AS `cairn.co.in`, in a `<span>`:
       * nothing to click, nothing in the tab order, no outbound anchor to rel-pair or announce. The
       * state is READ by everyone instead of hovered by some. `title` is still set, so the hover
       * Akhil asked for is there too — as a supplement to the visible text rather than the only
       * carrier of it.
       *
       * `aria-label` IS DELIBERATELY ABSENT on the span. On the anchors it replaces a nameless glyph;
       * here the visible text IS the name, and an `aria-label` would override it with a second
       * wording of the same thing.
       *
       * THE LABEL SAYS "Coming Soon" AND THE DATA SAYS "Play Store". Both are true and they answer
       * different questions — the record names the destination, the card names its state — so the
       * `title` carries the pair: "Play Store — Coming Soon".
       */
    }
    <ul class="wk-marks">
      {
        project.badges.map((badge) =>
          badge.pending === true ? (
            <li>
              <span
                class="wk-mark-link wk-mark-named wk-mark-pending"
                title={`${badge.label} ${EN_DASH} ${PENDING_LABEL}`}
              >
                <SocialIcons icon={badge.icon} size={16} />
                <span class="wk-mark-label">{PENDING_LABEL}</span>
              </span>
            </li>
          ) : (
            <li>
              <a
                class:list={['wk-mark-link', { 'wk-mark-named': badge.icon === 'globe' }]}
                href={badge.href}
                aria-label={`${badge.label} ${NEW_TAB}`}
                title={badge.label}
                target="_blank"
                rel="noopener noreferrer"
              >
                <SocialIcons icon={badge.icon} size={16} />
                {badge.icon === 'globe' && <span class="wk-mark-label">{badge.label}</span>}
              </a>
            </li>
          )
        )
      }
    </ul>
  </div>


</Card>
